---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---
<BaseLayout title="Student walkthroughs" description="Step-by-step instructions for editing a portfolio, using Git and GitHub, and publishing the site.">
<div class="container student-guide">
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="eyebrow">MIS CLUB · WORKSHOP 01 · STUDENT WALKTHROUGH</div>
    <h1>Build your portfolio.<br/><span class="hero-accent">Publish it with GitHub Pages.</span></h1>
    <p>Use this guide to set up the starter, update your portfolio, review your changes in Git, and publish the site. Each step includes commands and help for common errors.</p>
    <div class="guide-actions"><a class="btn primary" href="#step-1">Start with the setup <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><a class="btn" href={base+'designs/'}>Choose & download a theme ↓</a><a class="btn" href={base+'slides/'}>Open the slides ↗</a></div>
  </section>
  <section class="mission-map" aria-label="Portfolio setup steps">
    <div class="mission-map-head"><div><div class="eyebrow">WORKSHOP STEPS</div><h2>Use these steps to set up, edit, and publish your portfolio.</h2></div><p>Complete the steps in order, or skip ahead if your project already runs.</p></div>
    <div class="route-track" aria-label="Portfolio setup steps">
      <a class="route-stop" href="#step-1" data-route-step="1"><span class="route-node">01</span><b>Tools</b><small>Check setup</small></a>
      <a class="route-stop" href="#step-2" data-route-step="2"><span class="route-node">02</span><b>Create a repo</b><small>Set up GitHub</small></a>
      <a class="route-stop" href="#step-3" data-route-step="3"><span class="route-node">03</span><b>Run locally</b><small>Start the site</small></a>
      <a class="route-stop" href="#step-4" data-route-step="4"><span class="route-node">04</span><b>Edit</b><small>Update the content</small></a>
      <a class="route-stop" href="#step-5" data-route-step="5"><span class="route-node">05</span><b>Review</b><small>Open a pull request</small></a>
      <a class="route-stop" href="#step-6" data-route-step="6"><span class="route-node">06</span><b>Publish</b><small>Check the live URL</small></a>
    </div>
    <div class="route-rescue">
      <div><b>Need help with a step?</b><span>Choose what’s happening to find the next instructions.</span></div>
      <div class="rescue-options" role="group" aria-label="Choose your current situation">
        <button type="button" data-route-help="start">Starting fresh</button>
        <button type="button" data-route-help="local">Local site works</button>
        <button type="button" data-route-help="push">Push failed</button>
        <button type="button" data-route-help="actions">Deploy failed</button>
      </div>
      <p id="route-advice" aria-live="polite">Choose the problem that best matches what you see.</p>
      <a id="route-next-link" href="#step-1">Go to step 1 →</a>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="guide-status card">
    <div><div class="eyebrow">Progress tracker</div><h2 id="guide-progress-label">0 of 6 steps complete</h2><p class="muted small">Your checkpoints stay on this device. Nothing is sent to a server.</p></div>
    <div class="guide-progress"><div class="progress-wrap"><div class="progress-bar" id="guide-progress-bar"></div></div><span id="guide-progress-percent">0%</span></div>
  </section>

  <section class="guide-step" id="step-1" data-step="1">
    <div class="step-number">01</div><div class="step-content">
      <div class="step-kicker">STEP 01 · ACCESS + TOOLS</div><h2>Check your tools and accounts.</h2>
      <p class="step-lede">Check these tools before you start. If something is missing, install it now so you can focus on the project during the workshop.</p>
      <div class="tool-cards tool-cards-five">
        <article><b>GitHub</b><p>Repository hosting and collaboration in your browser.</p><a href="https://github.com" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open GitHub ↗</a></article>
        <article><b>Git</b><p>Local version history: commits, branches, and diffs.</p><a href="https://git-scm.com/downloads" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Install Git ↗</a></article>
        <article><b>VS Code</b><p>The editor where you change files and review diffs.</p><a href="https://code.visualstudio.com/download" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Install VS Code ↗</a></article>
        <article><b>Node.js + npm</b><p>Runs the Astro starter and installs its packages. Prefer Node.js 22 (what GitHub Actions uses). Node 18+ usually works for local preview.</p><a href="https://nodejs.org/en/download" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Install Node.js ↗</a></article>
        <article><b>Copilot</b><p>Optional assistant for explanations and focused code changes.</p><a href="https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/setup" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Copilot setup ↗</a></article>
      </div>
      <div class="tool-check">
        <div><span class="task-label">QUICK CHECK · OPEN VS CODE TERMINAL</span><pre id="tool-check-commands">git --version
node --version
npm --version</pre><button class="copy-button" id="copy-tool-check" type="button">Copy commands</button></div>
        <p><b>What you should see</b><br/>Three version numbers. Prefer <code>v22.x.x</code> to match Actions; <code>v18+</code> is usually fine locally. If a command says “not found,” use the install links above and restart the terminal.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="plain-note"><b>Useful words</b><p><strong>Repository (repo):</strong> project files plus history. <strong>Commit:</strong> a saved checkpoint. <strong>Publish:</strong> make the site available at a web address.</p></div>
      <div class="step-task"><span class="task-label">DO THIS</span><ol><li>Open your GitHub account in a browser.</li><li>Open VS Code on your computer.</li><li>In VS Code, check that the GitHub and Copilot accounts are the ones you plan to use.</li></ol><p class="help-text">If Copilot is not ready, keep going. You can follow the same steps without AI while the instructor helps.</p></div>
      <label class="step-done"><input type="checkbox" data-step-check="1"/> I can open GitHub and VS Code.</label>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="guide-step" id="step-2" data-step="2">
    <div class="step-number">02</div><div class="step-content">
      <div class="step-kicker">STEP 02 · CREATE A REPOSITORY</div><h2>Create your repository.</h2>
      <p class="step-lede">You’ll create your own copy of the starter so you can change it and publish it from your GitHub account.</p>
      <figure class="walkthrough-figure actual-product-screen"><div class="screen-label"><span>GITHUB · NEW REPOSITORY</span><a href="https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/creating-and-managing-repositories/quickstart-for-repositories" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Official documentation ↗</a></div><img src="https://docs.github.com/assets/images/help/repository/create-repository-name.png" alt="Screenshot from GitHub Docs showing the real new-repository form and Repository name field." loading="lazy" data-fallback={import.meta.env.BASE_URL+'walkthroughs/github-create-repository.svg'} onerror="this.onerror=null;this.src=this.dataset.fallback" /><figcaption>Real GitHub UI from the official docs. Open <a href="https://github.com/new" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">GitHub’s New repository page ↗</a>; the fields may differ slightly from this documentation screenshot.</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="step-task"><span class="task-label">DO THIS</span><ol><li>Open the <a href={base+'designs/'}>Portfolio lab</a> and download the theme ZIP you want (Editorial, Developer, or Professional). Each ZIP is a complete project with that theme already selected.</li><li>Extract the ZIP. You should see a folder such as <code>portfolio-editorial</code>. Open <strong>that</strong> folder in VS Code (<strong>File → Open Folder</strong>). Confirm you can see <code>package.json</code> and a <code>src</code> folder.</li><li>On GitHub, choose <strong>New repository</strong> (or <a href="https://github.com/new" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">open it directly ↗</a>). Give it a simple name such as <code>my-resume-site</code>.</li><li>Choose <strong>Public</strong>. Free GitHub Pages for student project sites requires a public repository. Only publish details you are happy to share.</li><li>Create the repository <strong>without</strong> adding a README, license, or .gitignore. The starter already has project files. Adding those now causes merge conflicts later.</li><li>On the empty repository page, copy the HTTPS URL (it looks like <code>https://github.com/YOUR-USERNAME/YOUR-REPOSITORY.git</code>). You will use it in the next step after the local preview works.</li></ol><p class="help-text">Do not push yet. First confirm the site runs on your computer in Step 3.</p></div>
      <div class="plain-note"><b>Why Public?</b><p>GitHub Pages for a normal project repository is free when the repository is <strong>Public</strong>. A Private repository usually cannot publish a free Pages site on a free student account.</p></div>
      <div class="plain-note"><b>Why this matters</b><p>Your repository will store the site and its history. You change it, review it, and publish it from your own GitHub account.</p></div>
      <label class="step-done"><input type="checkbox" data-step-check="2"/> I downloaded a theme ZIP and created an empty public GitHub repository.</label>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="guide-step" id="step-3" data-step="3">
    <div class="step-number">03</div><div class="step-content">
      <div class="step-kicker">STEP 03 · RUN THE PROJECT</div><h2>Run the project locally.</h2>
      <p class="step-lede">GitHub hosts the shared copy. In VS Code, you’ll open your local copy and run it before editing.</p>
      <figure class="walkthrough-figure actual-product-screen"><div class="screen-label"><span>VISUAL STUDIO CODE · SOURCE CONTROL</span><a href="https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/sourcecontrol/overview" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Official documentation ↗</a></div><img src="https://code.visualstudio.com/assets/docs/sourcecontrol/overview/overview.png" alt="Real VS Code interface with the Source Control view and a side-by-side file diff." loading="lazy" data-fallback={import.meta.env.BASE_URL+'walkthroughs/vscode-source-control.svg'} onerror="this.onerror=null;this.src=this.dataset.fallback" /><figcaption>Real VS Code screenshot. Select a changed file to read the diff before staging or committing it.</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="step-task"><span class="task-label">DO THIS · PREVIEW LOCALLY</span><ol><li>In VS Code, confirm the open folder contains <code>package.json</code> and the <code>src</code> folder.</li><li>Open the integrated terminal: <strong>Terminal → New Terminal</strong>.</li><li>Install the starter's packages with <code>npm install</code>. This downloads the tools the project uses.</li><li>Start a local preview with <code>npm run dev</code>.</li><li>Open the local address printed in the terminal, usually <code>http://localhost:4321</code>. You should see the sample portfolio for your chosen theme.</li></ol><p class="help-text">Leave the terminal running while you preview. The local address works on your computer; it is not the public website yet.</p></div>
<div class="command-grid command-grid-short">
 <div class="command-card"><div><b>Install project tools</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-npm-install">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-npm-install">npm install</pre></div>
 <div class="command-card"><div><b>Start the local preview</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-npm-dev">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-npm-dev">npm run dev</pre></div>
</div>
      <div class="prompt-card"><div class="prompt-heading"><div><span class="prompt-label">COPILOT PROMPT</span><h3>Ask Copilot to explain the project</h3></div><button class="copy-button" data-copy-prompt="explain-project">Copy prompt</button></div><pre id="explain-project">Explain this project in plain English. Tell me which file creates the page, which file controls the styles, and where I should edit my name, education, experience, skills, and projects. Do not change any files yet. Use short sentences.</pre></div>
      <div class="plain-note"><b>Read this before editing</b><p>It is okay if the code looks unfamiliar. First learn which file does what. Ask Copilot to explain; do not ask it to rewrite the entire project.</p></div>

      <div class="step-task"><span class="task-label">DO THIS · PUSH TO GITHUB</span><ol><li>Stop the preview with <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>C</kbd> in the terminal (or open a second terminal tab). On a Mac, that is still <strong>Control+C</strong>, not Command+C.</li><li>From the starter folder, run the commands below one at a time. Replace the uppercase placeholders with your GitHub username and repository name.</li></ol><p class="help-text">Copy one command at a time. Use the HTTPS URL from your empty repository page.</p></div>
<div class="command-grid">
 <div class="command-card"><div><b>1 · Start Git history</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-init">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-init">git init</pre></div>
 <div class="command-card"><div><b>2 · Stage the project files</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-add">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-add">git add .</pre></div>
 <div class="command-card"><div><b>3 · Inspect what is staged</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-first-diff">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-first-diff">git status
git diff --staged</pre><p class="help-text">Check for passwords, tokens, private files, or anything you do not want to publish.</p></div>
 <div class="command-card"><div><b>4 · Make the first commit</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-commit">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-commit">git commit -m "Create my portfolio"</pre></div>
 <div class="command-card"><div><b>5 · Name the main branch</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-branch">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-branch">git branch -M main</pre></div>
 <div class="command-card"><div><b>6 · Connect your GitHub repository</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-remote">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-remote">git remote add origin https://github.com/YOUR-USERNAME/YOUR-REPOSITORY.git</pre><p class="help-text">Change both capitalized placeholders first.</p></div>
 <div class="command-card"><div><b>7 · Send your files to GitHub</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-push">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-push">git push -u origin main</pre></div>
</div>
      <details class="terminal-details">
        <summary>Git says “Author identity unknown”?</summary>
        <p>Git stores a name and email with each commit. These identify the commit author; they are not your GitHub password. For a public portfolio, consider using the GitHub noreply address shown in <b>GitHub → Settings → Emails</b>.</p>
        <div class="command-grid command-grid-short">
          <div class="command-card"><div><b>Set your commit name</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-user-name">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-user-name">git config --global user.name "Your Name"</pre></div>
          <div class="command-card"><div><b>Set your commit email</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-user-email">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-user-email">git config --global user.email "YOUR-GITHUB-NOREPLY-EMAIL"</pre><p class="help-text">Replace the placeholder with your GitHub noreply email, then retry the commit.</p></div>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details class="terminal-details">
        <summary>Push asks for a password / authentication failed?</summary>
        <p>GitHub no longer accepts your normal account password for <code>git push</code> over HTTPS. Use one of these options:</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Browser sign-in (easiest on many laptops):</strong> when VS Code or Git opens a browser window, sign in and approve access, then retry <code>git push -u origin main</code>.</li>
          <li><strong>Personal Access Token:</strong> on GitHub go to <strong>Settings → Developer settings → Personal access tokens</strong>, create a classic token with the <code>repo</code> scope, then paste the token when the terminal asks for a password (username = your GitHub username).</li>
          <li><strong>Ask the facilitator</strong> if you are stuck for more than a few minutes — do not force-push.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="help-text">Official guide: <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/authentication/keeping-your-account-and-data-secure/creating-a-personal-access-token" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Creating a personal access token ↗</a></p>
      </details>
      <label class="step-done"><input type="checkbox" data-step-check="3"/> I previewed the site locally and pushed it to my GitHub repository.</label>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="guide-step" id="step-4" data-step="4">
    <div class="step-number">04</div><div class="step-content">
      <div class="step-kicker">STEP 04 · EDIT THE PORTFOLIO</div><h2>Personalize the portfolio.</h2>
      <p class="step-lede">Almost all content lives in one file: <code>src/data/profile.js</code>. Change that file, save it, and the local preview updates. Only include information you are comfortable making public.</p>
      <div class="student-designs">
        <article class="student-design"><div class="design-swatch editorial-swatch"><small>01 / EDITORIAL</small><b>Warm, editorial<br/>layout</b><i></i><i></i></div><strong>Editorial</strong><span>Photo + experience story layout.</span><div class="design-download"><a href={base+'previews/editorial/'}>Preview ↗</a><a href={base+'downloads/portfolio-editorial.zip'}>Download ZIP ↓</a></div></article>
        <article class="student-design"><div class="design-swatch developer-swatch"><small>02 / DEVELOPER</small><b>Projects first.<br/><em>Dark theme</em></b><i></i><i></i></div><strong>Developer</strong><span>Projects near the top.</span><div class="design-download"><a href={base+'previews/developer/'}>Preview ↗</a><a href={base+'downloads/portfolio-developer.zip'}>Download ZIP ↓</a></div></article>
        <article class="student-design"><div class="design-swatch professional-swatch"><small>03 / PROFESSIONAL</small><b>Two-column<br/>resume</b><i></i><i></i><i></i></div><strong>Professional</strong><span>Sidebar contact + main experience.</span><div class="design-download"><a href={base+'previews/professional/'}>Preview ↗</a><a href={base+'downloads/portfolio-professional.zip'}>Download ZIP ↓</a></div></article>
      </div>

      <figure class="explorer-figure" aria-label="Where to find profile.js in VS Code">
        <div class="screen-label"><span>VS CODE · EXPLORER</span><span>FIND THIS FILE</span></div>
        <div class="explorer-panel">
          <div class="explorer-sidebar" aria-hidden="true">
            <div class="explorer-title">EXPLORER</div>
            <ul class="explorer-tree">
              <li class="folder open">portfolio-editorial</li>
              <li class="indent folder open">public</li>
              <li class="indent2 file">photo.svg</li>
              <li class="indent2 file muted">PHOTO-README.txt</li>
              <li class="indent folder open">src</li>
              <li class="indent2 folder open">data</li>
              <li class="indent3 file active">profile.js</li>
              <li class="indent2 folder">layouts</li>
              <li class="indent2 folder">pages</li>
              <li class="indent file">package.json</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="explorer-copy">
            <p class="eyebrow">PATH TO OPEN</p>
            <p class="explorer-path"><code>src</code> → <code>data</code> → <code>profile.js</code></p>
            <p>In the left sidebar (Explorer), expand <strong>src</strong>, then <strong>data</strong>, then click <strong>profile.js</strong>. That single file holds your name, theme, experience, projects, and photo setting.</p>
            <p class="help-text">Optional photo files go in <code>public/</code> (for example <code>public/photo.jpg</code>), not inside <code>src/</code>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>

      <div class="step-task"><span class="task-label">DO THIS · OPEN THE CONTENT FILE</span>
        <ol>
          <li>In VS Code Explorer, open <code>src/data/profile.js</code> (see the path diagram above).</li>
          <li>Confirm <code>theme</code> matches the layout you want: <code>'editorial'</code>, <code>'developer'</code>, or <code>'professional'</code>. Change it if needed, then save.</li>
          <li>Replace <code>name</code>, <code>role</code>, <code>location</code>, <code>tagline</code>, <code>about</code>, <code>email</code>, <code>github</code>, and <code>linkedin</code> with your details.</li>
          <li>Update the <code>skills</code> list and the <code>education</code> object.</li>
          <li>Keep the local preview open (<code>npm run dev</code>) and refresh the browser after each save.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>

      <div class="prompt-card"><div class="prompt-heading"><div><span class="prompt-label">COPY · SWITCH THEME</span><h3>Theme line in profile.js</h3></div><button class="copy-button" data-copy-prompt="theme-line">Copy</button></div><pre id="theme-line">theme: 'developer',</pre></div>

      <div class="step-task"><span class="task-label">DO THIS · ADD EXPERIENCE</span>
        <ol>
          <li>Find the <code>experience: [ ... ]</code> list in <code>profile.js</code>.</li>
          <li>Edit an existing object, or copy one object and paste it below the previous one (keep the commas correct).</li>
          <li>Fill in <code>title</code>, <code>organization</code>, <code>date</code>, and <code>summary</code>.</li>
          <li>Delete sample roles you do not want. Jobs, campus roles, volunteering, and class leadership all count.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <div class="prompt-card"><div class="prompt-heading"><div><span class="prompt-label">COPY · EXPERIENCE OBJECT</span><h3>Paste inside the experience list</h3></div><button class="copy-button" data-copy-prompt="experience-object">Copy</button></div><pre id="experience-object">{'{'}
  title: 'Campus IT Helper',
  organization: 'University Help Desk',
  date: '2025–2026',
  summary: 'Answered student tech questions, reset accounts, and documented common fixes for the team.',
{'}'},</pre></div>

      <div class="step-task"><span class="task-label">DO THIS · ADD A PROJECT</span>
        <ol>
          <li>Find the <code>projects: [ ... ]</code> list.</li>
          <li>Edit one sample project or add a new object with <code>kind</code>, <code>title</code>, <code>summary</code>, <code>skills</code>, and <code>link</code>.</li>
          <li>Class assignments are fine if you describe your contribution honestly.</li>
          <li>Point <code>link</code> at a real GitHub repo, document, or demo when you have one.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <div class="prompt-card"><div class="prompt-heading"><div><span class="prompt-label">COPY · PROJECT OBJECT</span><h3>Paste inside the projects list</h3></div><button class="copy-button" data-copy-prompt="project-object">Copy</button></div><pre id="project-object">{'{'}
  kind: 'CLASS PROJECT',
  title: 'Inventory tracker',
  summary: 'Built a spreadsheet workflow that cut weekly stock-check time for a student organization.',
  skills: ['Excel', 'Documentation'],
  link: 'https://github.com/YOUR-USERNAME/YOUR-REPO',
{'}'},</pre></div>

      <div class="step-task"><span class="task-label">DO THIS · ADD A PHOTO (OPTIONAL)</span>
        <ol>
          <li>Save a clear headshot as <code>photo.jpg</code> (square crop works best).</li>
          <li>Drag that file into the project's <code>public/</code> folder in VS Code (same place as <code>PHOTO-README.txt</code>).</li>
          <li>In <code>src/data/profile.js</code>, set <code>photo: '/photo.jpg',</code> and save.</li>
          <li>Refresh the local site. Every theme shows the photo (or your initials if <code>photo</code> is empty).</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="help-text">To use initials instead: set <code>photo: '',</code></p>
      </div>
      <div class="prompt-card"><div class="prompt-heading"><div><span class="prompt-label">COPY · PHOTO LINE</span><h3>Photo setting in profile.js</h3></div><button class="copy-button" data-copy-prompt="photo-line">Copy</button></div><pre id="photo-line">photo: '/photo.jpg',</pre></div>

      <details class="terminal-details">
        <summary>Terminal shows SyntaxError after editing profile.js?</summary>
        <p>That usually means a missing or extra comma in the <code>experience</code> or <code>projects</code> list. Check these three things:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Every object except the last one in a list ends with <code>{'}'},</code> (comma after the closing brace).</li>
          <li>The last object in a list can end with <code>{'}'}</code> or <code>{'}'},</code> — both are fine — but do not leave a lone comma with nothing after it.</li>
          <li>Strings use matching quotes: <code>'like this'</code>.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="help-text">Undo the last edit (<strong>Edit → Undo</strong>), save, and run <code>npm run dev</code> again. If it still fails, ask the facilitator before rewriting the whole file.</p>
      </details>

      <div class="step-task"><span class="task-label">CHECK BEFORE YOU CONTINUE</span>
        <ol>
          <li>Preview at desktop width and at a narrow/mobile width.</li>
          <li>Click your email, GitHub, LinkedIn, and project links.</li>
          <li>Confirm the theme and photo look the way you expect.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>

      <figure class="walkthrough-figure actual-product-screen"><div class="screen-label"><span>VISUAL STUDIO CODE · COPILOT CHAT</span><a href="https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/chat/copilot-chat-context" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Official documentation ↗</a></div><img src="https://code.visualstudio.com/assets/docs/chat/copilot-chat/copilot-chat-view-chat-variables.png" alt="Real VS Code Copilot Chat screenshot showing the context picker for files and workspace context." loading="lazy" data-fallback={import.meta.env.BASE_URL+'walkthroughs/copilot-prompt.svg'} onerror="this.onerror=null;this.src=this.dataset.fallback" /><figcaption>Optional: use Copilot for one focused edit after you understand <code>profile.js</code>. Review every proposed change.</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="prompt-card"><div class="prompt-heading"><div><span class="prompt-label">COPILOT PROMPT</span><h3>Make one small improvement</h3></div><button class="copy-button" data-copy-prompt="one-improvement">Copy prompt</button></div><pre id="one-improvement">I am building a student resume portfolio in Astro. Please make one focused improvement: [describe the change]. Prefer editing src/data/profile.js when the change is content. Keep the existing project structure and theme. Do not add packages. First tell me which files you plan to change. After the change, explain what I should review and how to test it on mobile.</pre></div>
      <div class="plain-note"><b>Privacy check</b><p>Only publish details you are comfortable sharing publicly. Do not include your home address, passwords, API keys, private contact details, or information about another person without permission.</p></div>
      <label class="step-done"><input type="checkbox" data-step-check="4"/> My site includes my own information, experience or projects, and I checked the preview.</label>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="guide-step" id="step-5" data-step="5">
    <div class="step-number">05</div><div class="step-content">
      <div class="step-kicker">STEP 05 · REVIEW YOUR CHANGE</div><h2>Commit your change and open a pull request.</h2>
      <p class="step-lede">A commit saves a snapshot in your local history. A push sends it to GitHub. A pull request lets you review the change before merging it.</p>
      <div class="mini-workflow"><span>Issue</span><b>→</b><span>Branch</span><b>→</b><span>Commit</span><b>→</b><span>Pull request</span><b>→</b><span>Merge</span></div>
      <figure class="walkthrough-figure actual-product-screen"><div class="screen-label"><span>GITHUB · CREATE AN ISSUE</span><a href="https://docs.github.com/en/issues/tracking-your-work-with-issues/using-issues/creating-an-issue" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Official documentation ↗</a></div><img src="https://docs.github.com/assets/images/help/issues/issue-title-body.png" alt="Real GitHub issue creation form with title and body fields filled in." loading="lazy" data-fallback={import.meta.env.BASE_URL+'walkthroughs/github-issue.svg'} onerror="this.onerror=null;this.src=this.dataset.fallback" /><figcaption>Real GitHub issue form. Write what should change and how you will prove it is done.</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="step-task"><span class="task-label">DO THIS</span><ol><li>On GitHub, open <strong>Issues</strong> and create a small task. Example: “Add a featured project card.”</li><li>Write a few checks that tell you when it is complete: a title, a short summary, a working link, and a layout that fits a phone screen.</li><li>Create a branch for this task. A branch is a separate line of work; it keeps this change organized.</li><li>Make the change in VS Code. Inspect the diff, then write a clear commit message.</li><li>Push the branch to GitHub and open a pull request.</li></ol></div>
      <section class="git-state-lab" aria-labelledby="git-state-title">
        <div class="lab-heading"><div><div class="eyebrow">TRY THE GIT STEPS</div><h3 id="git-state-title">Understand what Git is tracking.</h3><p>Select a step to see what Git records and which command comes next.</p></div><span class="lab-status">LOCAL REPO</span></div>
        <div class="git-state-track" role="group" aria-label="Git change lifecycle">
          <button type="button" class="git-state-button is-current" data-git-state="0" aria-pressed="true"><span>01</span><b>Edited</b><small>Working tree</small></button>
          <span class="state-connector" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          <button type="button" class="git-state-button" data-git-state="1" aria-pressed="false"><span>02</span><b>Staged</b><small>Ready for commit</small></button>
          <span class="state-connector" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          <button type="button" class="git-state-button" data-git-state="2" aria-pressed="false"><span>03</span><b>Committed</b><small>Saved to history</small></button>
          <span class="state-connector" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          <button type="button" class="git-state-button" data-git-state="3" aria-pressed="false"><span>04</span><b>Pushed</b><small>Shared on GitHub</small></button>
        </div>
        <div class="git-state-detail">
          <div class="fake-terminal-real-meaning"><div class="terminal-chrome"><span>● ● ●</span><b>TERMINAL · EXAMPLE OUTPUT</b></div><pre id="git-state-output">On branch add-featured-project
Changes not staged for commit:
  modified: src/data/profile.js

no changes added to commit</pre></div>
          <div class="git-state-explanation"><span class="state-number" id="git-state-number">STATE 01 / 04</span><h4 id="git-state-heading">The edit exists only on your computer.</h4><p id="git-state-copy">Git can see the change, but it is not selected for the next commit.</p><div class="next-command"><div><span>NEXT COMMAND</span><code id="git-state-command">git add src/data/profile.js</code></div><button class="copy-button" type="button" id="copy-git-state">Copy command</button></div><p class="lab-small-note" id="git-state-note">Staging a file does not upload it to GitHub.</p></div>
        </div>
      </section>
      <details class="terminal-details"><summary>Optional: copy the Git commands for the branch</summary><p>Use these if you want to work in the terminal. You can also use the Source Control panel in VS Code. Copy one command at a time.</p><div class="command-grid command-grid-short">
        <div class="command-card"><div><b>Create your task branch</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-feature-branch">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-feature-branch">git switch -c add-featured-project</pre></div>
        <div class="command-card"><div><b>Check which files changed</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-status">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-status">git status</pre></div>
        <div class="command-card"><div><b>Stage the changes you reviewed</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-feature-add">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-feature-add">git add src/data/profile.js</pre></div>
        <div class="command-card"><div><b>Save a checkpoint</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-feature-commit">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-feature-commit">git commit -m "Add featured project card"</pre></div>
        <div class="command-card"><div><b>Push the branch to GitHub</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-feature-push">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-feature-push">git push -u origin add-featured-project</pre></div>
      </div></details>
      <figure class="walkthrough-figure actual-product-screen"><div class="screen-label"><span>GITHUB · REVIEW A PULL REQUEST</span><a href="https://docs.github.com/en/pull-requests/how-tos/review-pull-requests/reviewing-proposed-changes-in-a-pull-request" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Official documentation ↗</a></div><img src="https://docs.github.com/assets/images/help/pull_requests/diff-settings-menu.png" alt="Real GitHub pull request Files changed view and diff settings." loading="lazy" data-fallback={import.meta.env.BASE_URL+'walkthroughs/github-pull-request.svg'} onerror="this.onerror=null;this.src=this.dataset.fallback" /><figcaption>Real GitHub review interface. Open <b>Files changed</b>, read what changed, and compare the diff with your issue before you merge.</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="prompt-card"><div class="prompt-heading"><div><span class="prompt-label">COPILOT PROMPT</span><h3>Review your own change</h3></div><button class="copy-button" data-copy-prompt="review-change">Copy prompt</button></div><pre id="review-change">Review my current changes against these acceptance checks: [paste your checks]. Look for broken links, mobile layout problems, accessibility issues, accidental secrets, and unrelated edits. Explain any problems in plain English. Do not make more changes until I review your findings.</pre></div>
      <div class="plain-note"><b>Before you merge</b><p>Read the changed files. Make sure the change matches your issue. Do not merge only because a button is green or Copilot says it is finished.</p></div>
      <label class="step-done"><input type="checkbox" data-step-check="5"/> I recorded a change and reviewed a pull request (or followed the class checkpoint).</label>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="guide-step" id="step-6" data-step="6">
    <div class="step-number">06</div><div class="step-content">
      <div class="step-kicker">STEP 06 · PUBLISH THE SITE</div><h2>Deploy and verify the live site.</h2>
      <p class="step-lede">GitHub Actions builds the site, and GitHub Pages publishes it. If the build fails, open the failed job and read the log before changing files.</p>
      <div class="deploy-illustration"><article><span>01</span><b>Your repository</b><small>Astro source files</small></article><div>→</div><article><span>02</span><b>GitHub Actions</b><small>Install + build</small></article><div>→</div><article><span>03</span><b>GitHub Pages</b><small>Live website</small></article></div>
      <figure class="walkthrough-figure actual-product-screen"><div class="screen-label"><span>GITHUB · PAGES SOURCE</span><a href="https://docs.github.com/en/pages/getting-started-with-github-pages/configuring-a-publishing-source-for-your-github-pages-site" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Official documentation ↗</a></div><img src="https://docs.github.com/assets/images/help/pages/publishing-source-drop-down.png" alt="Real GitHub Pages settings showing the publishing source dropdown." loading="lazy" data-fallback={import.meta.env.BASE_URL+'walkthroughs/github-pages.svg'} onerror="this.onerror=null;this.src=this.dataset.fallback" /><figcaption>Real GitHub Pages UI. Under <strong>Build and deployment → Source</strong>, choose <strong>GitHub Actions</strong> for this starter.</figcaption></figure>
      <figure class="walkthrough-figure actual-product-screen"><div class="screen-label"><span>GITHUB · PAGES PUBLISHED URL</span><a href="https://docs.github.com/en/pages/getting-started-with-github-pages/configuring-a-publishing-source-for-your-github-pages-site" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Official documentation ↗</a></div><img src="https://docs.github.com/assets/images/help/pages/click-pages-url-to-preview.png" alt="Real GitHub Pages settings and Visit site link for a published website." loading="lazy" data-fallback={import.meta.env.BASE_URL+'walkthroughs/github-pages.svg'} onerror="this.onerror=null;this.src=this.dataset.fallback" /><figcaption>Real GitHub Pages UI. Follow the published URL only after the latest build and deployment have succeeded.</figcaption></figure>
      <figure class="walkthrough-figure actual-product-screen"><div class="screen-label"><span>GITHUB ACTIONS · JOB OUTPUT</span><a href="https://docs.github.com/en/actions/how-tos/monitor-workflows/use-workflow-run-logs" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Official documentation ↗</a></div><img src="https://docs.github.com/assets/images/help/repository/actions-quickstart-log-detail.png" alt="Real GitHub Actions log view with an expanded step and output." loading="lazy" data-fallback={import.meta.env.BASE_URL+'walkthroughs/github-actions-run.svg'} onerror="this.onerror=null;this.src=this.dataset.fallback" /><figcaption>Real Actions log view. Open the failed job, expand the failed step, and read the first actionable error before editing code.</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="step-task"><span class="task-label">DO THIS</span><ol><li>On your repository, open <strong>Settings</strong>, then <strong>Pages</strong>.</li><li>Under <strong>Build and deployment → Source</strong>, choose <strong>GitHub Actions</strong>. Do this even if an Actions run already started.</li><li>Open the <strong>Actions</strong> tab and find the latest <strong>Build and deploy portfolio</strong> run.</li><li>If the first run failed because Pages was not enabled yet, open that run → <strong>Re-run all jobs</strong>.</li><li>Wait until build and deploy are green.</li><li>Return to <strong>Settings → Pages</strong> and open the published URL (often <code>https://YOUR-USERNAME.github.io/YOUR-REPOSITORY/</code>).</li><li>Check the homepage, your photo (if you added one), one project link, and a narrow/mobile window.</li></ol><p class="help-text">The first deploy can take a few minutes. If it fails, open the run, expand the failed step, and read the first clear error before changing files.</p></div>
      <div class="prompt-card"><div class="prompt-heading"><div><span class="prompt-label">COPILOT PROMPT</span><h3>Understand a deployment error</h3></div><button class="copy-button" data-copy-prompt="debug-deployment">Copy prompt</button></div><pre id="debug-deployment">Help me understand this build/deployment error in plain English: [paste the error text]. Tell me which step failed, the likely cause, and the smallest safe fix. Do not guess at settings that are not shown in the log.</pre></div>
      <label class="step-done"><input type="checkbox" data-step-check="6"/> My published website opens, or I know exactly which deployment step needs help.</label>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="finish-card">
    <div class="eyebrow">END OF WORKSHOP 01</div><h2>Your portfolio is published.</h2><p>You edited a file, saved the change in Git, pushed it to GitHub, and published the site. In Workshop 2, you’ll add automated checks. Keep this guide nearby; the same steps apply to future changes.</p>
    <p class="finish-demo">Behind or comparing notes? Finished catch-up demo: <a href="https://github.com/skyeshanohan/workshop1-demo-portfolio" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">repo ↗</a> · <a href="https://skyeshanohan.github.io/workshop1-demo-portfolio/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">live site ↗</a></p>
    <p class="finish-demo">Want a fuller resume example with Student / Emerging / Professional layouts? <a href="https://tyler-beach.github.io/example-my-resume-site/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">tyler-beach.github.io/example-my-resume-site ↗</a> · <a href="https://github.com/tyler-beach/example-my-resume-site" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">repo ↗</a></p>
    <div class="finish-links"><a class="btn primary" href={base+'workshop/'}>Open the workshop agenda ↗</a><a class="btn" href={base+'slides/'}>Open the presentation ↗</a></div>
  </section>
</div>
